WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.036 --> 00:00:03.921
Hey guys, I am super excited today, because

00:00:03.921 --> 00:00:07.810
I hadn't made such a cool,
multilingual video in a long time.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.701
I have a very special guest today,

00:00:09.701 --> 00:00:11.139
we spoke a bunch of languages

00:00:11.139 --> 00:00:13.264
about a bunch of topics,

00:00:13.264 --> 00:00:16.845
and there is a &lt;i&gt;bonus&lt;/i&gt; at the end
of the video recording to watch

00:00:16.845 --> 00:00:20.001
and the other interesting piece of news today

00:00:20.001 --> 00:00:23.705
is that we haven't made just one video,
we have made TWO different videos,

00:00:23.705 --> 00:00:26.531
one for my channel, one for his channel.

00:00:26.531 --> 00:00:30.034
So if you like what you are going to hear and see

00:00:30.034 --> 00:00:34.018
please go to the description box
and in the comments section,

00:00:34.018 --> 00:00:36.661
and click on the link
and you will enjoy

00:00:36.661 --> 00:00:39.203
another very cool,
multilingual video

00:00:39.203 --> 00:00:40.247
Enjoy the show!

00:00:41.027 --> 00:00:43.367
Hi guys, hi from sunny Rome,

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:45.615
here is Luca, from Luca Lampariello.com and

00:00:45.615 --> 00:00:49.699
I am here with the legendary Stefano Suigo,

00:00:49.699 --> 00:00:51.637
Stefano, how is it going today?

00:00:51.637 --> 00:00:54.151
Stefano: I am good, what about you?

00:00:54.151 --> 00:00:55.415
Luca: I am great, fantastic,

00:00:55.415 --> 00:00:56.729
the weather is great,

00:00:56.729 --> 00:00:57.831
couldn't be better!

00:00:57.831 --> 00:01:00.163
For all those..for the few left in the world

00:01:00.163 --> 00:01:01.659
who don't know Stefano Suigo, Stefano,

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:07.633
is (laughter) a fantastic polyglot,
he speaks a bunch of languages

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:08.981
How many languages do you speak, Stefano?

00:01:08.981 --> 00:01:11.504
Stefano: Well, let's say 10+2

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:13.209
Luca: 10+2

00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:17.217
Ok, I guess you speak 10 fluently
and 2 you are learning, right?

00:01:17.217 --> 00:01:19.210
Stefano: Kind of, yes

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.228
Luca: And..Stefano and I been thinking of
making videos for a couple 'years

00:01:26.228 --> 00:01:29.649
and we met a couple of times,

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:32.573
I think only 2 times at the Polyglot Gathering

00:01:32.573 --> 00:01:34.722
and at some other language events,

00:01:34.722 --> 00:01:36.600
we hit it off very well,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.629
and I think it was about time to make a video

00:01:39.629 --> 00:01:41.757
about some interesting stuff

00:01:41.757 --> 00:01:43.708
and most of all about language learning,

00:01:43.708 --> 00:01:45.980
but not only about language learning.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.779
We have posted some stuff on FB and Instagram

00:01:48.779 --> 00:01:50.629
and we have received a lot of questions

00:01:50.629 --> 00:01:52.519
both on his channel and on my channel

00:01:52.519 --> 00:01:56.598
and here we are going to talk about a few of them

00:01:56.598 --> 00:02:00.460
in a few languages with a surprise at the end.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.723
So, without further ado, let's get started and

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:08.062
let's address the very first question.
Ok?

00:02:08.062 --> 00:02:08.658
Ok

00:02:08.658 --> 00:02:09.939
Are you ready?

00:02:09.939 --> 00:02:11.091
Stefano: Yeah I am!

00:02:11.091 --> 00:02:14.453
And thank you for having me, it's just
a privilege to be with you here today

00:02:14.453 --> 00:02:17.778
Luca: It is pleasure, 
the pleasure is all mine.

00:02:17.778 --> 00:02:23.037
So, let's get started, every question 
is going to be in English

00:02:23.037 --> 00:02:26.763
but then we are going to decide which
language we are going to speak in and reply...

00:02:26.763 --> 00:02:31.259
The first question..Stefano you can reply first and then I'm gonna go later, OK?

00:02:31.259 --> 00:02:35.958
So..speak from day 1 or wait?

00:02:35.958 --> 00:02:39.362
I wanted to stress the fact that we
have condensed some questions

00:02:39.362 --> 00:02:43.388
and we are going to thank each and
everyone of you at the end of the video personally

00:02:43.388 --> 00:02:49.044
but we have condensed questions because
a lot of questions gravitate around the same topics

00:02:49.044 --> 00:02:50.488
so we just made them short.

00:02:50.488 --> 00:02:52.723
Speak from day 1 or wait?

00:02:52.723 --> 00:02:55.593
So in other words, do you think it is a good idea to

00:02:55.593 --> 00:02:58.136
when you first learn a language from scratch

00:02:58.136 --> 00:03:00.644
to start from the very beginning or

00:03:00.644 --> 00:03:04.652
you think you should wait until you get
the language in you and then speak?

00:03:04.652 --> 00:03:05.839
What do you think?

00:03:05.839 --> 00:03:09.527
Stefano you are going to reply,
if you want, in French [&lt;i&gt;en français&lt;/i&gt;]

00:03:09.527 --> 00:03:12.124
[in French] Stefano: In French, we're going
to speak French, first...

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:13.530
Hello!

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:22.707
So, well, for me, it depends a lot
on what you mean by "speak from day 1".

00:03:22.707 --> 00:03:24.369
Isn't it?

00:03:24.369 --> 00:03:28.176
If it's just about producing sounds,

00:03:28.176 --> 00:03:34.549
imitating the pronunciation,
to do the best you can do,

00:03:34.549 --> 00:03:37.403
then yes, that's something I do
right from the very first day,

00:03:37.403 --> 00:03:39.897
when I start learning a new language,

00:03:39.897 --> 00:03:42.274
because, for me, it is crucial

00:03:42.274 --> 00:03:45.084
to learn the pronunciation from the get-go

00:03:45.084 --> 00:03:52.014
and so I will start producing sounds,
trying to imitate native speakers,

00:03:52.014 --> 00:03:55.686
I'll try to create, build,
some basic sentences,

00:03:55.686 --> 00:03:57.105
and speak out loud,

00:03:57.105 --> 00:03:58.890
read out loud as well

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:02.215
So, if speaking from day 1 means that,
yes, that's what I do

00:04:02.215 --> 00:04:11.623
but that does not mean that I am going to
look for a language partner

00:04:11.623 --> 00:04:18.382
or even an online tutor
from the very first day or week.

00:04:18.382 --> 00:04:28.610
No, I do it only after a preliminary phase
of autonomous learning

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.369
which can be more or less long,

00:04:31.377 --> 00:04:33.545
depending on the language

00:04:33.545 --> 00:04:39.202
I mean the distance between
my native language,

00:04:39.202 --> 00:04:42.973
or the other languages
that I already master,

00:04:42.973 --> 00:04:45.277
and my new target language.

00:04:45.277 --> 00:04:46.951
Depending on this,

00:04:46.951 --> 00:04:49.648
it can last 1, 2,
even 3 months,

00:04:49.648 --> 00:04:51.389
e.g. for Georgian

00:04:51.389 --> 00:04:54.950
because Georgian is a very
different language

00:04:54.950 --> 00:05:00.532
from all the ones I have learned so far

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:07.688
So...yes, the goal is to reach a level

00:05:07.688 --> 00:05:15.366
which allows you to hold a real 
communication, a real conversation

00:05:15.366 --> 00:05:19.675
and not just "hi", "how's it going?"
and "thanks"

00:05:19.675 --> 00:05:24.886
because that's not enough
to have a real conversation

00:05:24.886 --> 00:05:30.119
and most of all,
it does not allow you

00:05:30.119 --> 00:05:33.548
me to learn much
from this conversation

00:05:33.548 --> 00:05:39.777
I have to be able to at least
learn something new,

00:05:39.777 --> 00:05:44.807
understand my tutor's corrections for instance

00:05:44.807 --> 00:05:50.917
and to give something
to my language partner as well

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:54.549
So I would say, in conclusion,

00:05:54.549 --> 00:05:59.205
you have to indeed start from the beginning,
to produce the language

00:05:59.205 --> 00:06:04.633
Because a language is oral production,
mostly, for me

00:06:04.633 --> 00:06:09.946
but through speaking to oneself

00:06:09.946 --> 00:06:11.750
Luca: Excellent, excellent...

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:15.600
Stefano: ...and on the other hand,
whether to start speaking to other people

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.068
depends on the distance between languages.

00:06:19.068 --> 00:06:23.027
It can last up to 3, 4 months.

00:06:23.027 --> 00:06:25.337
Luca: Can you give a concrete example
of what happened

00:06:25.337 --> 00:06:27.218
as you were learning a specific language

00:06:27.218 --> 00:06:30.369
that took you a long time
and another one that did not

00:06:30.369 --> 00:06:34.073
as it was a &lt;i&gt;relatively&lt;/i&gt; easy language?

00:06:34.073 --> 00:06:38.126
Stefano: Well... I would say,
with French...

00:06:38.126 --> 00:06:41.294
(laugh) French is a language that,
actually,

00:06:41.294 --> 00:06:43.232
I have learned without ever studying it

00:06:43.232 --> 00:06:49.689
so I have learned the core of the language without...

00:06:49.689 --> 00:06:57.115
without waiting for long
before having my first conversations

00:06:57.115 --> 00:07:01.609
I live here, in Bruxelles.
It is a francophone city.

00:07:01.609 --> 00:07:06.540
And it is here, on the street,
that I have practically learned French

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:10.184
On the other hand, as I said, Georgian...

00:07:10.184 --> 00:07:13.286
It is such a different
and complex language...

00:07:13.286 --> 00:07:17.801
That I wanted to be sure to at least

00:07:17.801 --> 00:07:23.371
understand the corrections
and the reason of these corrections

00:07:23.371 --> 00:07:28.380
before launching myself
into a conversation

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:33.180
Otherwise, it would not
have made that much sense

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:35.614
to just say "hello" and "thanks"

00:07:35.614 --> 00:07:37.223
Luca: Definitely, definitely,

00:07:37.223 --> 00:07:39.720
Actually, I think it depends,

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.687
Personally, I think,

00:07:41.687 --> 00:07:47.621
there is a variety of different
positions, opinions about what to do,

00:07:47.621 --> 00:07:49.893
whether to speak from the get-go,
or wait,

00:07:49.893 --> 00:07:52.228
Personally I think, in the end,
it depends,

00:07:52.228 --> 00:07:55.633
If one observes children,
one could draw the conclusion

00:07:55.633 --> 00:07:58.235
that you need to wait 2, 3, 4...5 years

00:07:58.235 --> 00:07:59.640
But we're not children!

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:01.128
We already have a language,

00:08:01.128 --> 00:08:03.388
and we might start speaking it
from the very beginning

00:08:03.388 --> 00:08:05.688
I personally think it depends.

00:08:05.688 --> 00:08:09.944
Germans have a funny answer.

00:08:09.944 --> 00:08:13.297
They say "jein"
[ja=yes / nein=no]

00:08:13.297 --> 00:08:17.950
It's neither "ok", nor "no".
I think it depends on the person.

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:22.686
I think we can refer to two
categories of factors

00:08:22.686 --> 00:08:26.309
that are major,
and play an important role

00:08:26.309 --> 00:08:28.409
The first one is an objective factor

00:08:28.409 --> 00:08:31.140
As you said, there are languages,
whose distance

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:33.713
- Personally I like this
linguistic concept: distance

00:08:33.713 --> 00:08:35.752
It's not that a language is
difficult or not

00:08:35.752 --> 00:08:40.746
it is, that it takes &lt;i&gt;more&lt;/i&gt; time
if a language is distant

00:08:40.746 --> 00:08:42.933
from a linguistic point of view

00:08:42.933 --> 00:08:45.695
Like, suppose I were italian,

00:08:45.695 --> 00:08:47.477
- well, actually, I am -

00:08:47.477 --> 00:08:50.169
as an Italian, going to Japan,

00:08:50.169 --> 00:08:51.707
- you talk about "distance" -

00:08:51.707 --> 00:08:54.183
for me, learning Japanese,
which is distant from Italian,

00:08:54.183 --> 00:08:56.024
is just like going from Rome
to Japan

00:08:56.024 --> 00:08:59.445
And going to Spain is not that...

00:08:59.445 --> 00:09:02.705
It's right over here,
2 hours long by plane

00:09:02.705 --> 00:09:04.621
1 hour if you go to Barcelona
from Rome

00:09:04.621 --> 00:09:08.887
and the same goes
for the linguistic distance

00:09:08.887 --> 00:09:10.534
Spanish and Italian

00:09:10.534 --> 00:09:12.399
Italian and French

00:09:12.399 --> 00:09:14.337
Italian and Portuguese

00:09:14.337 --> 00:09:17.329
It's two languages
whose distance is mi...

00:09:17.329 --> 00:09:18.930
- well, I wouldn't say &lt;i&gt;minimal&lt;/i&gt; -

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.122
So, there is not that much
distance to cover

00:09:21.122 --> 00:09:24.931
And thus, it takes &lt;i&gt;less&lt;/i&gt; time

00:09:24.931 --> 00:09:27.528
That is one &lt;i&gt;objective&lt;/i&gt; factor.

00:09:27.528 --> 00:09:29.764
But then, you have the individual learner.

00:09:29.764 --> 00:09:31.688
That is, there are individuals

00:09:31.688 --> 00:09:34.745
who have a lot of experience,
or less experience

00:09:34.745 --> 00:09:37.148
it depends on the individual's experience,
on time,

00:09:37.148 --> 00:09:40.316
on whether you're retired,
- then you have a lot of time

00:09:40.316 --> 00:09:42.214
whereas other people have less time

00:09:42.214 --> 00:09:45.085
It depends on motivation,
it depends on where you live

00:09:45.085 --> 00:09:48.807
it depends on a bunch of factors,
that depend on the individual,

00:09:48.807 --> 00:09:50.522
So I, personally, would say

00:09:50.522 --> 00:09:52.682
if there is a person that 
comes to me and say

00:09:52.682 --> 00:09:54.570
"Luca, should I speak right away?"

00:09:54.570 --> 00:09:56.292
I say, it depends,
it depends on you,

00:09:56.292 --> 00:10:00.361
Sometimes it's good,
sometimes it's less useful

00:10:00.361 --> 00:10:03.158
In my case,
when I, for instance,

00:10:03.158 --> 00:10:05.921
started learning Portuguese,
in 2018

00:10:05.921 --> 00:10:08.034
- sorry, in 2008*

00:10:08.034 --> 00:10:11.339
Um... No, it was 2010, actually...
I can't remember...

00:10:11.339 --> 00:10:13.105
No, 2008! 2008!

00:10:13.105 --> 00:10:14.866
With Mandarin

00:10:14.866 --> 00:10:18.164
Well, I waited for months and months
before speaking

00:10:18.164 --> 00:10:20.593
I realized I could've spoken
from the get-go

00:10:20.593 --> 00:10:23.156
I could have spoken after 3 months

00:10:23.156 --> 00:10:26.325
I was doing Assimil,
very slowly,

00:10:26.325 --> 00:10:29.106
But the truth is,
after just 2, 3 months,

00:10:29.106 --> 00:10:30.937
I could speak it already

00:10:30.937 --> 00:10:32.383
For Catalan is the same thing, I think.

00:10:32.383 --> 00:10:34.845
If I were to begin Catalan,
some day soon,

00:10:34.845 --> 00:10:37.452
I'm sure I would start,
not necessarily speaking it,

00:10:37.452 --> 00:10:38.833
I would need 1 or 2 months,

00:10:38.833 --> 00:10:41.285
But I think in that case,
it's an advantage to speak from the get-go

00:10:41.285 --> 00:10:43.459
Because you already have
many languages in you

00:10:43.459 --> 00:10:46.777
and the language isn't complicated, so...
It depends. For me, it depends.

00:10:46.777 --> 00:10:52.129
There are factors that are, say, objective
and others subjective.

00:10:52.129 --> 00:10:53.942
Luca: So... I wanted to add this.
Stefano: Yes, absolutely.

00:10:53.942 --> 00:10:58.671
For me, the same example 
would be Dutch

00:10:58.671 --> 00:11:02.140
If I were to start Dutch,
probably, I...

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:07.958
I wouldn't wait for 3 months before
having my first conversations.

00:11:07.958 --> 00:11:10.797
Luca: Exactly. Exactly.

00:11:10.797 --> 00:11:13.459
Luca: For me, for instance, Dutch...
Stefano: on the first week or...

00:11:13.459 --> 00:11:18.006
Luca: Dutch, personally,
I started to learn it in...1999

00:11:18.006 --> 00:11:21.719
And I realized, after 3 months, I...
I wrote a letter

00:11:21.719 --> 00:11:23.955
- because I met...a girl -

00:11:23.955 --> 00:11:27.409
I write a letter for her.
And I realized after...

00:11:27.409 --> 00:11:29.586
- Ya, it's always about &lt;i&gt;chicks&lt;/i&gt;
["meufs", common slang for girls,
less vulgar than "chicks" and not derogatory]

00:11:29.586 --> 00:11:32.021
I realized that after 3 months, I...

00:11:32.021 --> 00:11:33.713
I &lt;i&gt;could&lt;/i&gt; write in Dutch,

00:11:33.713 --> 00:11:36.958
because it's very similar to German

00:11:36.958 --> 00:11:43.423
even though Germans can't understand Dutch
while the Dutch can understand German.

00:11:43.423 --> 00:11:45.671
Anyway, that's a different story...

00:11:45.671 --> 00:11:49.622
So, that was the first question
I have the list of questions -

00:11:49.622 --> 00:11:52.989
We have a second question , I am going 
to say it in English and in Italian

00:11:52.989 --> 00:11:55.427
It was asked originally in Italian

00:11:56.967 --> 00:12:00.321
I'll say it in Italian first of all and then I'll translate it into English

00:12:00.321 --> 00:12:02.844
[in Italian] Since you guys are both Italian, 
I'd love to know

00:12:02.844 --> 00:12:05.650
which common points do you have 
when it comes to language learning

00:12:05.650 --> 00:12:06.967
- you laugh already -

00:12:06.967 --> 00:12:09.087
and which you find absolutely necessary -

00:12:09.087 --> 00:12:13.043
the "Polyglot's Commandments"

00:12:13.043 --> 00:12:16.786
so the question is, since you are both Italian, 
I want to know

00:12:16.786 --> 00:12:26.066
the most important features, 
the most important things

00:12:26.066 --> 00:12:28.382
you have to stick to, you have to abide by,

00:12:28.382 --> 00:12:33.386
that a native Italian polyglot has to abide by

00:12:33.386 --> 00:12:37.373
I think this goes for any polyglot
regardless of the origin.

00:12:37.373 --> 00:12:39.341
And there's an insight question,

00:12:39.341 --> 00:12:41.478
there's another question related to that.

00:12:41.478 --> 00:12:45.415
Which is: I'd like to hear you talking Italian
about what polyglots believe to be

00:12:45.415 --> 00:12:48.934
the most essential elements 
for achieving verbal fluency.

00:12:48.934 --> 00:12:51.598
This is a little bit more specific,
for verbal fluency.

00:12:51.598 --> 00:12:54.848
We are gonna reply to both questions.

00:12:54.848 --> 00:12:57.992
[in Italian] And, as I was saying earlier, 
you can start Stefano

00:12:57.992 --> 00:13:00.519
and then I will complete, ok?

00:13:00.519 --> 00:13:03.461
Stefano: Of course, first of all, 
how nice talking

00:13:03.461 --> 00:13:05.217
with you in Italian, which is the language

00:13:05.217 --> 00:13:08.233
that we've always been using since we met

00:13:08.233 --> 00:13:10.309
actually, it is weird to use other languages

00:13:10.309 --> 00:13:12.573
we've never spoken other languages

00:13:12.573 --> 00:13:14.597
other than English maybe

00:13:14.597 --> 00:13:18.442
By the way, answering the question

00:13:18.442 --> 00:13:21.070
I believe that the first "commandment",

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:23.493
even if it is quite strange to talk 
about commandments,

00:13:23.493 --> 00:13:25.422
The first golden rule I think is not just

00:13:25.422 --> 00:13:27.547
common to the two of us,

00:13:27.547 --> 00:13:32.234
but to the majority of
polyglots and language lovers,

00:13:32.234 --> 00:13:34.085
is motivation.

00:13:34.085 --> 00:13:41.282
Maybe the most important factor 
is the motivation

00:13:41.282 --> 00:13:48.031
and knowing why we are studying 
a certain language

00:13:48.031 --> 00:13:53.783
I think that this awareness, 
or this voice that tells you why,

00:13:53.783 --> 00:13:58.852
have to accompany you all the way 
through your learning.

00:13:58.852 --> 00:14:03.961
It is not just something you start with 
and then you forget about

00:14:03.961 --> 00:14:11.470
because there will always be moments 
where you'll have "set backs"

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:14.022
How is it in Italian...

00:14:14.022 --> 00:14:17.733
Luca: You have second thoughts, doubts...

00:14:17.733 --> 00:14:22.143
Stefano: Thank you, exactly. 
Or let's say moments of difficulty

00:14:22.143 --> 00:14:24.034
when you don't know where to go

00:14:24.034 --> 00:14:27.785
and this awareness, in my opinion, 
that is somewhere in your brain

00:14:27.785 --> 00:14:34.052
will help you with new momentum,
to overcome these moments.

00:14:34.052 --> 00:14:38.991
The second big factor, personally,
is fun.

00:14:38.991 --> 00:14:42.465
I only do things that I like.

00:14:42.465 --> 00:14:53.477
It might sound weird, but rarely, I do something
language-related that I don't like doing.

00:14:53.477 --> 00:14:58.094
The enjoyment, doing something because you want to,

00:14:58.094 --> 00:15:01.251
and not because you have to,

00:15:01.251 --> 00:15:06.458
is what allows me to learn more efficiently.

00:15:06.458 --> 00:15:09.590
I mean, with the same amount of time

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:12.601
I learn more efficiently

00:15:12.601 --> 00:15:16.423
If I do something that amuses me,
for example,

00:15:16.423 --> 00:15:19.965
If I speak or read about something that I enjoy,

00:15:19.965 --> 00:15:27.057
and that I would read or listen to in Italian too,
not just in the target language

00:15:27.057 --> 00:15:30.294
So this is fundamental for me.

00:15:30.294 --> 00:15:33.945
Another thing, another golden rule,
is that...

00:15:33.945 --> 00:15:36.772
I don't know if you already heard about this,

00:15:36.772 --> 00:15:40.397
but "one is more than zero",
One is way more than zero

00:15:40.397 --> 00:15:44.215
One not just a little bit more,
is a lot more than zero

00:15:44.215 --> 00:15:48.048
Luca: It's your motto.
Stefano: This is my motto, yes.

00:15:48.048 --> 00:15:50.800
Meaning that, especially for those who have a family,

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:57.703
is a dad, etc... the time, I can assure you,
time grows short.

00:15:57.703 --> 00:16:00.814
As soon as you have five minutes,

00:16:00.814 --> 00:16:03.260
you should not think 
"oh no, just 5 minutes is not worth it"

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:05.337
instead, yes, it is worth it.

00:16:05.337 --> 00:16:08.314
Even just doing one simple thing,

00:16:08.314 --> 00:16:13.177
listening or building to a sentence,
making a thought, anything...

00:16:13.177 --> 00:16:21.084
Any exposure to the language, is way much better
than not doing anything.

00:16:21.084 --> 00:16:27.156
So, even if you just have 5 minutes,
don't procrastinate, but do that something

00:16:27.156 --> 00:16:37.835
because that is way more valuable than what it seems.
And... finally, If I may something else

00:16:37.835 --> 00:16:42.335
that maybe you also said many times, in many of your videos.

00:16:42.335 --> 00:16:47.800
And that is to open the doors of your own life,

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:50.404
to the language that you want to learn.

00:16:50.404 --> 00:16:58.279
This means surrounding yourself with tools and
inputs in the foreign language.

00:16:58.279 --> 00:17:07.725
Set up your life, so that you almost "have to"
use the language, without noticing it.

00:17:07.725 --> 00:17:13.924
Without doing extra efforts for using it.

00:17:13.924 --> 00:17:23.232
I'm talking about meetings, apps that allow you
to meet people from all over the world...

00:17:23.232 --> 00:17:28.620
you can chat with people in China, in Japan, without any problem.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:35.683
You can talk on Skype, you can look for a tutor or a
language partner.

00:17:35.683 --> 00:17:41.779
You can subscribe to magazines about something
interesting for you,

00:17:41.779 --> 00:17:47.175
and that you would use
in your mother tongue anyway.

00:17:47.175 --> 00:17:50.818
You can do the same with a foreign language

00:17:50.818 --> 00:17:54.529
What I do here, in Brussels, which is a very international city,

00:17:54.529 --> 00:18:01.146
is going to shops or cafes, where the owners
or the people that go there

00:18:01.146 --> 00:18:04.465
speak the language you're interested in.

00:18:04.465 --> 00:18:10.336
You hang out with them and speak that language
Every day even, if you want

00:18:10.336 --> 00:18:14.370
Any time you can go to the cafe and talk
for example, in Portuguese

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:16.085
here in the cafe next door

00:18:16.085 --> 00:18:19.314
This is the secret

00:18:19.872 --> 00:18:22.363
Also for maintaining the languages

00:18:22.363 --> 00:18:25.477
not just for learning them
this is fundamental.

00:18:25.477 --> 00:18:27.406
At the beginning of this year,

00:18:27.406 --> 00:18:39.808
one morning, at midday, I realized I had
already used 8 languages.

00:18:39.808 --> 00:18:43.400
It doesn't happen every day,
it is an exception.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:48.023
But I didn't actively try to use all those languages,

00:18:48.023 --> 00:18:53.429
and, not even at 12 pm, I had already spoken
8 languages.

00:18:53.429 --> 00:18:55.902
Among which, Georgian, because I found out

00:18:55.902 --> 00:19:00.883
that the mother of two kids that go to school
with mine, was Georgian.

00:19:00.883 --> 00:19:03.962
So, I even spoke Georgian at the bus station.

00:19:03.962 --> 00:19:07.402
Luca: When you use Georgian that the bus station
it means that you won

00:19:07.402 --> 00:19:09.900
Stefano: The day could end there!

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:14.408
Luca: Before 12!! 
You can go back home happy!

00:19:14.408 --> 00:19:17.770
You're great Stefano, as always.

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:23.688
So, as for me, there are some things
that overlap.

00:19:23.688 --> 00:19:25.593
You've already said some of them

00:19:25.593 --> 00:19:27.739
So I'm going to tell you what I think

00:19:27.739 --> 00:19:31.520
You already said the most important one

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:34.734
But I'm going to say it again because is fundamental
is the WHY.

00:19:34.734 --> 00:19:39.236
The motivation comes from the why,
knowing why you do something.

00:19:39.236 --> 00:19:42.150
Very often, the people that give up

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:49.238
don't know that what usually happens
in any new activity,

00:19:49.238 --> 00:19:52.943
is that in the first week you'll have a lot of
enthusiasm...

00:19:52.943 --> 00:19:54.828
"I'll do this, I'll do that"

00:19:54.828 --> 00:19:59.261
For languages, you start a sport, you go swimming...
Any activity...

00:19:59.261 --> 00:20:04.380
And then you drammatically lose motivation and moment

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:07.119
Which is completely natural.
Many books talk about it.

00:20:07.119 --> 00:20:10.742
And that's the moment where having
clear ideas make a big difference.

00:20:10.742 --> 00:20:13.134
Why do you want to start a new language?

00:20:13.134 --> 00:20:17.783
In particular, before starting a new language
I always think about it very well.

00:20:17.783 --> 00:20:20.228
There are always these "sirens" calling me

00:20:20.228 --> 00:20:23.971
They often ask me "why did you learn this
language, or this other one?"

00:20:23.971 --> 00:20:26.727
And I always answer that it's not me 
the one who choose the language,

00:20:26.727 --> 00:20:29.152
but it is the language that chooses me.

00:20:29.152 --> 00:20:33.880
Somehow it leaves hints.

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:38.931
I went for a trip somewhere, 
I like the country, I read a literary work

00:20:38.931 --> 00:20:45.280
I met an interesting person, I saw a book 
that suddenly made me explore this country

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:47.238
You name it...

00:20:47.238 --> 00:20:53.100
At that point, it is important to understand
that watching a movie or meeting a person

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:57.271
Is maybe necessary for me, but not enough

00:20:57.271 --> 00:21:05.109
for overcoming the obstacles that we will
inevitably meet on our way.

00:21:05.109 --> 00:21:09.792
So, knowing the why, 
knowing exactly why it is important,

00:21:09.792 --> 00:21:13.300
if creates motivation in the long term.

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:16.120
Another important thing for me,

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:19.598
I'm not saying it is generic, 
but particularly for me,

00:21:19.598 --> 00:21:22.801
I noticed, reflecting on myself,
that it is fundamental for me...

00:21:22.801 --> 00:21:27.181
I always consciously visualize,

00:21:27.181 --> 00:21:30.287
in the beginning and during 
the first months or weeks,

00:21:30.287 --> 00:21:34.243
how I will be in the future, 
in the next two years

00:21:34.243 --> 00:21:36.638
talking and using that language 
in the future.

00:21:36.638 --> 00:21:41.654
And every single time I sit down 
to study a language,

00:21:41.654 --> 00:21:44.269
be it Greek, Hungarian, Danish...

00:21:44.269 --> 00:21:48.931
I project myself, my mind looks ahead
automatically and says:

00:21:48.931 --> 00:21:52.975
"How beautiful it will be speaking 
or using this language"

00:21:52.975 --> 00:21:57.783
For me, the language "doesn't live
in a vacuum", as native English speakers would put it.

00:21:57.783 --> 00:21:59.615
"it doesn't live in a vacuum"

00:21:59.615 --> 00:22:02.477
But it's an live entity.

00:22:02.477 --> 00:22:06.365
So, as opposed to other polyglots
or language lovers,

00:22:06.365 --> 00:22:11.101
I don't...no criticism intended but

00:22:11.101 --> 00:22:14.748
I never see a language as something
that I can learn &lt;i&gt;a little bit&lt;/i&gt;

00:22:14.748 --> 00:22:16.983
If I start it, there is no going back!

00:22:16.983 --> 00:22:20.630
It's a life mission.

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:24.490
Learning a language for me is about Integrating it into my life,
to change it, to enrich it.

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:26.602
It's not just about saying simple things such as "Yes" and "hello".

00:22:26.602 --> 00:22:29.768
Saying even just a word to a foreigner in his language

00:22:29.768 --> 00:22:31.160
fills them with joy.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:32.793
But for me it is like a mission.

00:22:32.793 --> 00:22:35.779
I know that you told me the same
thing yesterday,

00:22:35.779 --> 00:22:38.180
we agree on this.

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:40.932
Third thing...
Second thing,

00:22:40.932 --> 00:22:44.445
so, let's say, looking ahead
and knowing why,

00:22:44.445 --> 00:22:45.733
motivate yourself is fundamental.

00:22:45.733 --> 00:22:48.150
Without this, we don't go anywhere.

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:50.435
It is like having a Ferrari without gasoline

00:22:50.435 --> 00:22:53.457
You can have the best method in the world but without fuel, you are not going anywhere

00:22:53.457 --> 00:22:55.641
The second important thing is to plan.

00:22:55.641 --> 00:23:00.661
I mean, I'm quite structured,
as I showed you yesterday.

00:23:00.661 --> 00:23:03.906
Maybe even too structured,
I mean excessive.

00:23:03.906 --> 00:23:06.461
You don't need to be so structured.

00:23:06.461 --> 00:23:08.497
By the way, I'm sorry guys.

00:23:08.497 --> 00:23:12.163
There are those green parrots 
that make,

00:23:12.163 --> 00:23:19.550
Every like 20 seconds there's this sound
Stefano: It's beautiful! Don't worry!

00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:22.270
Luca: I hope so!

00:23:22.270 --> 00:23:24.777
They are beautiful to look at, but hearing them is something else altogether

00:23:24.777 --> 00:23:28.511
After five hours with this chirping..it is a bit too much.

00:23:28.511 --> 00:23:32.663
Anyway, the second thing is to plan

00:23:32.663 --> 00:23:35.948
It is something I teach to my students:

00:23:35.948 --> 00:23:39.959
to distinguish the planning phase from the actual learning phase.

00:23:39.959 --> 00:23:43.930
So, if you plan on, say Saturdays and Sundays what you will do during the following week

00:23:43.930 --> 00:23:46.763
Then, during the following week, you only have to sit down and make it happen.

00:23:46.763 --> 00:23:54.359
I recently read that Steve Jobs 
in his biography, I don't remember which one

00:23:54.359 --> 00:24:00.375
was famous for always wearing the same clothes.

00:24:00.375 --> 00:24:04.429
And he said that the reason for this was

00:24:04.429 --> 00:24:09.436
he didn't have to put energy in thinking
what to wear in the morning.

00:24:09.436 --> 00:24:12.907
For me, this is very similar
because on Saturday and Sunday

00:24:12.907 --> 00:24:14.971
I plan my whole week

00:24:14.971 --> 00:24:22.386
So that I don't have to choose 
what to do, how and when during the week.

00:24:22.386 --> 00:24:26.654
And it is fundamental especially when 
you have to manage 14 languages.

00:24:26.654 --> 00:24:30.500
So, the more structured you are, the better

00:24:30.500 --> 00:24:38.285
if you want to have a certain 
"polyglot lifestyle".

00:24:38.285 --> 00:24:42.487
So, my suggestion, also for the people
who are not polyglots,

00:24:42.487 --> 00:24:45.321
is to structure your day.

00:24:45.321 --> 00:24:49.492
Knowing exactly what to do is an extra.

00:24:49.492 --> 00:24:53.198
Then you can relax and do what 
you think is best.

00:24:53.198 --> 00:24:58.465
So, this is another important point,
as far as I'm concerned.

00:24:58.465 --> 00:25:02.587
And third, there are many more,

00:25:02.587 --> 00:25:08.318
but third is to invest your time
to understand what's the best method

00:25:08.318 --> 00:25:11.896
for you for learning a language.

00:25:11.896 --> 00:25:16.913
This means that there are some fundamental 
principles that are valid for everybody

00:25:16.913 --> 00:25:18.611
But the methods are different

00:25:18.611 --> 00:25:22.914
and maybe you and I have reached 
a good level of “polyglotism”

00:25:22.914 --> 00:25:26.722
Let’s call it this way - Jeez, it almost sounds like a disease!

00:25:26.722 --> 00:25:30.145
But we have quite different 
styles and methods.

00:25:30.145 --> 00:25:33.823
which means that you do stuff
that is good for you

00:25:33.823 --> 00:25:35.712
And I do things that are good for me

00:25:35.712 --> 00:25:41.681
And you must like what you are doing.

00:25:41.681 --> 00:25:45.436
Every time that I sit down, 
I have so much fun!

00:25:45.436 --> 00:25:52.880
Unfortunately, after those 30-45 minutes 
I have to go back to my chores

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:57.470
But the reality is that I could stay 
the entire day, learning.

00:25:57.470 --> 00:25:59.115
Because it so much fun for me.

00:25:59.115 --> 00:26:03.836
So, don’t look at a language like something 
that you must do and is boring.

00:26:03.836 --> 00:26:06.831
We have inherited this from school.

00:26:06.831 --> 00:26:13.137
So, school is important 
but talking about languages,

00:26:13.137 --> 00:26:18.200
you can learn however you want 
and having fun,

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:19.860
I can assure you that.

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:22.917
Stefano: Two things, if I can add.
Luca: Of course!

00:26:22.917 --> 00:26:27.130
Stefano: Some advice I give 
to my students when they say

00:26:27.130 --> 00:26:29.844
“I have to study more vocabulary”

00:26:29.844 --> 00:26:31.929
“I have to study more the conjunctive”

00:26:31.929 --> 00:26:32.940
“I have to study…”

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:34.960
I say: “You have to, or you want to?”

00:26:34.960 --> 00:26:39.149
Let’s start saying 
“I want to learn Italian better"

00:26:39.149 --> 00:26:41.991
"I want to learn Finnish grammar”

00:26:41.991 --> 00:26:47.780
And it’s just about mindset 
but it gives you a different momentum.

00:26:47.780 --> 00:26:50.515
Starting saying “I want to learn this”

00:26:50.515 --> 00:26:53.881
instead of “I have to learn this”

00:26:53.881 --> 00:27:01.483
You hear that those are two different ways 
of putting yourself in front of it.

00:27:01.483 --> 00:27:06.265
And the second thing is that there’s 
a big difference between the two of us.

00:27:06.265 --> 00:27:09.482
I basically never plan anything.

00:27:09.482 --> 00:27:20.170
I could use a bad word, but let’s say 
that I follow my feelings, my sensations.

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:25.613
But I still have a structure, because, 
without it, you’ll go nowhere.

00:27:25.613 --> 00:27:30.971
Structure for me is the book 
that I start learning with,

00:27:30.971 --> 00:27:36.419
that’s the skeleton that allows me 
to keep learning

00:27:36.419 --> 00:27:42.309
And then I have a limited 
number of resources,

00:27:42.309 --> 00:27:45.211
like an online vocabulary,

00:27:45.211 --> 00:27:50.943
two or three YouTube channels 
for the videos,

00:27:50.943 --> 00:27:54.900
one podcast that I like for the audio.

00:27:54.900 --> 00:28:01.400
But for me planning too much 
would be counterproductive,

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:03.509
because of how I am.

00:28:03.509 --> 00:28:08.576
I mean, as you said, 
there is not a universal way

00:28:08.576 --> 00:28:12.486
but everyone has to find their own way.

00:28:12.486 --> 00:28:17.313
Luca: It takes all sorts to make a world!

00:28:17.313 --> 00:28:18.626
[in English] Ok, guys, we're back.

00:28:18.626 --> 00:28:22.462
Sorry, I had to move from the balcony
because the sound was not optimal.

00:28:22.462 --> 00:28:26.830
We had beautiful light, 
but it was getting in the way

00:28:26.830 --> 00:28:30.102
So, we are back and we're gonna continue with
the questions

00:28:30.102 --> 00:28:32.814
So, the next one is in German.

00:28:34.817 --> 00:28:37.860
Are you still motivated to learn?

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:40.387
If yes, what makes you want

00:28:40.387 --> 00:28:43.652
to keep going? And of course, that's

00:28:43.652 --> 00:28:45.978
an interesting question. We've already

00:28:45.978 --> 00:28:48.300
spoken about that some, but

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:49.848
again, Stefano...

00:28:49.848 --> 00:28:52.529
Stefano: Yes, so...
Luca: You can share your opinion.

00:28:52.529 --> 00:28:54.987
Stefano: That's a super good question.

00:28:54.987 --> 00:28:58.196
I think in my case it's above all

00:28:58.196 --> 00:29:02.680
the fun of the language itself.

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:04.708
Maybe that's a bit different

00:29:04.708 --> 00:29:06.835
than how you see it, but

00:29:06.835 --> 00:29:08.714
for me, the fun is in the language itself

00:29:08.714 --> 00:29:10.800
with its characteristics,

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:14.798
unique sounds, the intonation, and

00:29:14.798 --> 00:29:19.344
all the structures and possibilities for

00:29:19.344 --> 00:29:22.354
expressing oneself, which are
always different, as well as the

00:29:22.354 --> 00:29:27.520
connection between the language and
culture of a country, all of that.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:34.212
And to observe, or experience for oneself,
how all of that comes together

00:29:34.212 --> 00:29:38.877
from zero to fluent, and almost becomes second nature.

00:29:38.877 --> 00:29:41.593
That's so fascinating to me.

00:29:41.593 --> 00:29:44.226
And that's different for each language,

00:29:44.226 --> 00:29:47.650
so each language is a new adventure

00:29:47.650 --> 00:29:51.616
and that motivates me
to learn more languages.

00:29:51.616 --> 00:29:55.128
There's always
something new to discover.

00:29:55.128 --> 00:30:02.700
And then, of course, the countless friends I can talk to

00:30:02.700 --> 00:30:06.107
when I can finally speak a new language.

00:30:06.107 --> 00:30:13.199
But also, for me this is especially important, 
during the actual process of learning.

00:30:13.199 --> 00:30:19.382
I don't just think "When am I finally going to speak that language fluently",

00:30:19.382 --> 00:30:26.517
but instead the learning in
and of itself is fun for me.

00:30:26.517 --> 00:30:28.195
Luca: Yep!

00:30:28.195 --> 00:30:30.756
Stefano: That makes it simpler.

00:30:30.773 --> 00:30:35.189
So yeah, and even during 
the process of learning

00:30:35.189 --> 00:30:39.234
I look forward to deep
conversations with other people,

00:30:39.234 --> 00:30:46.413
to the access to content that wouldn't
be available to me otherwise.

00:30:46.413 --> 00:30:49.729
What else...

00:30:49.729 --> 00:30:55.559
sometimes there's also a special
feeling of belonging,

00:30:55.559 --> 00:31:01.703
so, the joy of being in a group and feel like
part of the group,

00:31:01.703 --> 00:31:05.582
that's something very special.

00:31:05.582 --> 00:31:07.516
That also motivates me further.

00:31:07.516 --> 00:31:12.177
Other than that, yes, of course the fun
and the success that I've had

00:31:12.177 --> 00:31:15.559
with language learning up to now,

00:31:15.559 --> 00:31:17.570
of course I want to keep going,

00:31:17.570 --> 00:31:21.371
and I don't want to stop discovering
new worlds.

00:31:21.371 --> 00:31:27.826
Yes, and that's more or less...
Luca: That's everything!

00:31:27.826 --> 00:31:30.703
Stefano: ...my answer! And what about you?

00:31:30.703 --> 00:31:32.669
Luca: I had a small question for you.

00:31:32.669 --> 00:31:36.781
You said that you love
this "grammar stuff",

00:31:36.781 --> 00:31:39.617
or the small details in general, 
the small details in a language,

00:31:39.617 --> 00:31:41.725
and I can imagine that

00:31:41.725 --> 00:31:44.815
not everyone loves foreign languages
like you do, right?

00:31:44.815 --> 00:31:48.762
But yeah, I thought I love 
these small details,

00:31:48.762 --> 00:31:53.832
but I have to wonder if
you're actually a "grammar freak",

00:31:53.832 --> 00:31:55.874
in English one says
"grammar freak",

00:31:55.874 --> 00:31:58.974
and you love the
grammar of a language.

00:31:58.974 --> 00:32:03.341
Do you love- do you like, well, 
not only the grammar,

00:32:03.341 --> 00:32:08.310
but also learning the grammatical
rules every day?

00:32:08.310 --> 00:32:11.406
Stefano: Well, not necessarily every day, but

00:32:11.406 --> 00:32:14.400
that's definitely part of a language...
Luca: Not every day! But...

00:32:14.400 --> 00:32:19.204
yeah, that's a part of the language
that has significance for me.

00:32:19.204 --> 00:32:22.223
Luca: That's what-
Stefano: "Grammar enthusiast"! One can say that.

00:32:22.223 --> 00:32:26.570
Luca: Okay, that's also a motivation
sometimes, right?

00:32:26.570 --> 00:32:27.590
Stefano: Yes.

00:32:27.590 --> 00:32:30.947
Luca: In my case...we already said that we're
a little different, that's completely

00:32:30.947 --> 00:32:36.363
normal, we have similarities, but there
are also things that we do differently.

00:32:36.363 --> 00:32:38.709
In my case, I have to say, the people

00:32:38.709 --> 00:32:42.315
that think "Oh, a polyglot, he has to
love grammar!",

00:32:42.315 --> 00:32:47.336
in my case, I'm not sure 
if that's true, I love grammar,

00:32:47.336 --> 00:32:51.705
I know that it's important,
that it plays an important role,

00:32:51.705 --> 00:32:53.875
and you certainly have 
to learn the grammar

00:32:53.875 --> 00:32:57.125
in order to speak a language well, to
express yourself well,

00:32:57.125 --> 00:33:04.735
but on the other hand I don't know if I
love it entirely.

00:33:04.735 --> 00:33:09.618
What I believe motivates me the most is

00:33:09.618 --> 00:33:12.779
the possibility of using a language

00:33:12.779 --> 00:33:15.733
sooner or later with native

00:33:15.733 --> 00:33:17.768
speakers, right? We already said that,

00:33:17.768 --> 00:33:19.554
I can imagine, every time that

00:33:19.554 --> 00:33:24.230
I sit down at my desk, I begin to

00:33:24.230 --> 00:33:29.517
think, right? About how my life will

00:33:29.517 --> 00:33:32.180
be in the future. I don't know, I can

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:34.581
imagine - I did that in German, in

00:33:34.581 --> 00:33:38.177
Dutch, or when I learned other

00:33:38.177 --> 00:33:42.428
languages - I imagined "Ah okay, I'll be

00:33:42.428 --> 00:33:45.149
sitting on a beach, chatting with a girl."

00:33:45.149 --> 00:33:47.626
Stefano: Yes, of course!

00:33:47.626 --> 00:33:51.786
Luca: Of course, yes! Chatting with a

00:33:51.786 --> 00:33:56.302
cute girl in this language, but of course

00:33:56.302 --> 00:33:58.863
it's not only about girls, right?

00:33:58.886 --> 00:34:00.725
To have interesting conversations about

00:34:00.725 --> 00:34:05.272
the culture, the history of a country.

00:34:05.272 --> 00:34:10.165
Or to sit at a table with interesting

00:34:10.165 --> 00:34:13.535
people and to talk about the traditions

00:34:13.535 --> 00:34:16.333
of a country, or not only the traditions,

00:34:16.333 --> 00:34:18.142
the culture, the movies, the

00:34:18.142 --> 00:34:21.365
books, whatever. It's unbelievable!

00:34:21.365 --> 00:34:26.260
And I have experienced so many good and

00:34:26.260 --> 00:34:28.514
unbelievable experiences in this

00:34:28.514 --> 00:34:31.616
sense, and I'm thankful, so thankful.

00:34:31.616 --> 00:34:37.274
And I know what it means to speak a

00:34:37.274 --> 00:34:39.326
foreign language well, not only to babble

00:34:39.326 --> 00:34:42.431
a little bit, but to be able

00:34:42.431 --> 00:34:47.340
to use a language with native speakers.

00:34:47.340 --> 00:34:49.956
And I also have to say, in my life, I've

00:34:49.956 --> 00:34:53.820
lived in France and Spain, and to know a

00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:56.513
language, to speak a language well and

00:34:56.513 --> 00:35:02.145
fluently, if you travel to a country and

00:35:02.145 --> 00:35:06.204
you definitely have to live in this

00:35:06.204 --> 00:35:08.351
country, it's very important to speak a

00:35:08.351 --> 00:35:12.515
language. Okay, in Sweden, maybe in

00:35:12.515 --> 00:35:14.531
Scandinavia it's a bit different, but

00:35:14.531 --> 00:35:17.858
in France, in Spain, in Italy, people

00:35:17.858 --> 00:35:19.567
usually say "Oh yeah, you only know

00:35:19.567 --> 00:35:21.609
English, if you speak English everything
will be fine."

00:35:21.609 --> 00:35:24.990
That's not true. In France, in

00:35:24.990 --> 00:35:27.919
Spain, in Italy, and it's not only a

00:35:27.919 --> 00:35:29.925
question of communication

00:35:29.925 --> 00:35:31.399
"You should speak with the people."

00:35:31.399 --> 00:35:34.882
It's that, if you don't speak the

00:35:34.882 --> 00:35:39.180
language, everything goes (whistling) -

00:35:39.180 --> 00:35:43.213
You don't see the culture, the details of

00:35:43.213 --> 00:35:45.941
the culture, it's not the same
experience, right?

00:35:45.941 --> 00:35:47.521
Yeah.

00:35:47.521 --> 00:35:49.839
And therefore that's what motivates

00:35:49.839 --> 00:35:54.432
me the most. For example, Hungarian:

00:35:54.432 --> 00:36:00.238
Many people, including some Hungarians, ask me

00:36:00.238 --> 00:36:03.909
"Luca, why are you learning Hungarian?

00:36:03.909 --> 00:36:06.201
Why? You don't live in Hungary, you don't

00:36:06.201 --> 00:36:10.709
have..." for example, many students, I

00:36:10.709 --> 00:36:13.443
spoke with my teacher, she told me, many

00:36:13.443 --> 00:36:17.148
students are people that are just

00:36:17.148 --> 00:36:22.510
originally from Hungary, maybe their

00:36:22.510 --> 00:36:28.887
parents were from Hungary, or something

00:36:28.887 --> 00:36:31.818
like that, and maybe they want to learn

00:36:31.818 --> 00:36:33.370
the original language.

00:36:33.370 --> 00:36:36.660
"But you, you're Italian, you don't live
in Hungary!"

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:37.864
And I keep saying

00:36:37.864 --> 00:36:43.307
"I can imagine, in the future, 
I'm interested in living in Hungary."

00:36:43.307 --> 00:36:48.771
I was in Budapest and it was so cool and
I'll definitely use the language

00:36:48.771 --> 00:36:51.698
in my life. That motivates me.

00:36:51.698 --> 00:36:57.842
All of that motivates me, to use a language 
in order to improve myself,

00:36:57.842 --> 00:37:04.209
but also in this world, in order to 
"live better" in this world,

00:37:04.209 --> 00:37:06.962
and live these experiences. That's all.

00:37:06.962 --> 00:37:10.300
I spoke too much, sorry!

00:37:12.243 --> 00:37:14.793
So, next question here...
I have the list here

00:37:14.793 --> 00:37:17.638
is "Daily habits and actions"

00:37:17.638 --> 00:37:20.549
I think they wanna know our 
daily habits and actions

00:37:20.549 --> 00:37:23.586
not necessarily of polyglots 
but good language learners

00:37:23.586 --> 00:37:27.715
[in Portuguese] and we're going to answer
in Portuguese

00:37:27.715 --> 00:37:30.310
Stefano: Ah, we are finally 
speaking Portuguese

00:37:30.310 --> 00:37:35.433
Luca: we speak European Portuguese
I like it very much...

00:37:35.433 --> 00:37:39.526
but well, this doesn't matter but

00:37:39.526 --> 00:37:46.037
people generally ask me why I do 
European Portuguese

00:37:46.037 --> 00:37:51.314
as if it weren't normal to speak it, 
OK, what are you gonna do?

00:37:51.314 --> 00:37:58.621
Stefano: Alright, so, let's talk about 
our habits during normal times

00:37:58.621 --> 00:38:02.162
and not during this Corona crisis

00:38:02.162 --> 00:38:06.583
because we do not want to talk about that
we want to talk about other things

00:38:06.583 --> 00:38:12.087
So let's talk about what I do everyday
in normal times

00:38:12.087 --> 00:38:14.873
So, I have a family, I have kids

00:38:14.873 --> 00:38:21.605
therefore my life is also father life,
a large part of my life is as a father

00:38:21.605 --> 00:38:27.178
so I usually get up at 6.20-6.30
in the morning everyday

00:38:27.178 --> 00:38:29.708
Luca: Oh, ok, early

00:38:29.708 --> 00:38:31.093
Stefano: Excuse me?
Luca: Early!

00:38:31.093 --> 00:38:38.501
Stefano: Yeah, early, pretty early
not as early as you but... [laughter]

00:38:38.501 --> 00:38:42.346
and then I prepare breakfast 
for the whole family

00:38:42.346 --> 00:38:45.633
I also prepare some snacks for the 
kids to take to school

00:38:45.633 --> 00:38:53.465
then after breakfast I bring them
to the bus stop (European version)

00:38:53.465 --> 00:39:00.216
or to the bus stop (Brazilian version) 
for all those who speak Brazilian PT

00:39:00.216 --> 00:39:05.423
and when I come back home, 
at 8 o'clock, my work day starts

00:39:05.423 --> 00:39:12.192
I have lessons or I work on other projects
all related to languages

00:39:12.192 --> 00:39:17.210
and around 11.30 I usually stop working

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:20.876
and I start studying, 
generally the new language

00:39:20.876 --> 00:39:23.264
currently that's Georgian, you know

00:39:23.264 --> 00:39:27.145
and I try to do at least 30 minutes

00:39:27.145 --> 00:39:32.700
it can be reading, grammar, 
it can be whatever

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:38.918
After lunch I usually watch 
a couple of videos in languages that

00:39:38.918 --> 00:39:43.367
I have a hard time finding opportunities

00:39:43.367 --> 00:39:52.050
to practise in everyday life

00:39:52.050 --> 00:39:58.743
so, such languages will be Chinese, 
Japanese, and Romanian too

00:39:58.743 --> 00:40:04.793
after that I go back to work
usually until 4 pm

00:40:04.793 --> 00:40:10.484
because my kids come back from school,
so... that's it! [laughter]

00:40:10.484 --> 00:40:18.132
My working day, the time I have for myself
is over, and from then on

00:40:18.132 --> 00:40:22.543
it's 100% father time until bedtime

00:40:22.543 --> 00:40:30.147
in the evenings I like to read something, 
for some minutes before falling asleep

00:40:30.147 --> 00:40:39.590
I am currently reading short stories 
in Icelandic, they're really cool

00:40:39.590 --> 00:40:44.595
Luca: In what? In Icelandic?
Stefano: Yes, exactly

00:40:44.595 --> 00:40:47.254
Luca: Ah, yoiu speak Icelandic as well...

00:40:47.254 --> 00:40:50.360
Stefano: That's stories, short stories.

00:40:50.360 --> 00:40:56.076
Then I also have books in Japanese, Romanian...
in short, I read something

00:40:56.076 --> 00:41:01.614
usually literature, but sometimes 
even Georgian grammar. And why is that?

00:41:01.614 --> 00:41:04.785
Because Georgian grammar 
is great to fall asleep! [laughter]

00:41:04.785 --> 00:41:09.910
Luca: For sure!

00:41:09.910 --> 00:41:17.333
Stefano: If one day you suffer from 
sleeplessness, you can be sure

00:41:17.333 --> 00:41:20.279
Georgian grammar is gonna be 
your remedy

00:41:20.279 --> 00:41:25.211
Luca: Awesome, simply awesome!
Stefano: It's a solution.

00:41:25.211 --> 00:41:32.758
So that's my day. 
Other than that, I always try to schedule

00:41:32.758 --> 00:41:37.455
at least one or two hours of conversation
lessons on Italki

00:41:37.455 --> 00:41:41.114
with people from all around the world

00:41:41.114 --> 00:41:47.728
in order to practise the languages that
I normally do not use everyday

00:41:47.728 --> 00:41:51.339
Luca: Two hours a day???

00:41:51.339 --> 00:41:55.421
Stefano: Did I say "a day"? No, no,
a week, a week

00:41:55.421 --> 00:41:58.764
Luca: Ah, two hours a week
Stefano: I beg your pardon, no, no,

00:41:58.764 --> 00:42:05.267
it's one or two hours a week...
That's all, so what about your day?

00:42:05.267 --> 00:42:14.896
Luca: Very well. I get up at 5am,
pretty early :)

00:42:14.896 --> 00:42:19.353
Stefano: That's pretty early indeed 
Luca: Pretty early but I like it

00:42:19.353 --> 00:42:25.466
I like it because I then prepare 
breakfast, I medidate

00:42:25.466 --> 00:42:28.420
then I write my logbook

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:33.091
it's very important for me to write
for at least 10 minutes a day

00:42:33.091 --> 00:42:36.604
to write down what's in my head:
ideas, and stuff

00:42:36.604 --> 00:42:43.004
and then for me it's very important 
to read a book everyday for 30 minutes

00:42:43.004 --> 00:42:50.708
I'm currently reading a book called
"Thinking fast and slow"

00:42:50.708 --> 00:42:53.978
I don't know if you've heard of it, 
it's very interesting

00:42:53.978 --> 00:42:59.803
then I study one language for half an hour

00:42:59.803 --> 00:43:07.414
currently I'm learning Hungarian, Greek
and Danish

00:43:07.414 --> 00:43:13.273
I can't study 3 languages at the same time
right now because unfortunately

00:43:13.273 --> 00:43:16.472
I don't have the time for all 3 everyday

00:43:16.472 --> 00:43:21.021
and then I work, I do creative work,

00:43:21.021 --> 00:43:28.329
that is writing articles, preparing videos
for my YouTube channel and other things

00:43:28.329 --> 00:43:37.956
then I make lunch, and for me it is
important to sleep a bit

00:43:37.956 --> 00:43:42.545
an hour in the afternoon, otherwise
it's impossible

00:43:42.545 --> 00:43:50.765
at night I only sleep 6-7 hours
but sleeping is very very important for me

00:43:50.765 --> 00:43:55.929
and then I give lessons
because I'm a coach

00:43:55.929 --> 00:44:00.625
3 or 4 hours a day,
that's a lot of work too

00:44:00.625 --> 00:44:08.805
Ah, an important thing to say is that 
after lunch I read for 30 minutes

00:44:08.805 --> 00:44:17.101
I always read in the mornings, afternoons 
and evenings, for 30 minutes each

00:44:17.101 --> 00:44:23.299
three different books,
for example, in the morning I read

00:44:23.299 --> 00:44:25.299
slightly more difficult books, because

00:44:25.299 --> 00:44:29.778
"Thinking fast and slow" you can't read
in the evening, it's too complex

00:44:29.778 --> 00:44:35.184
after lunch another book about 
self-development

00:44:35.184 --> 00:44:41.159
which is a key topic for me:
understanding how to do things

00:44:41.159 --> 00:44:43.062
how to organize things

00:44:43.062 --> 00:44:46.749
and then in the evenings, I don't know, 
stories for example

00:44:46.749 --> 00:44:53.099
I am reading a very interesting book 
that I had already read a long time ago

00:44:53.099 --> 00:44:59.971
in Italian - I'm reading it in Italian -
that's "Sofia's World", maybe you know it

00:44:59.971 --> 00:45:01.888
Stefano: I've heard of it
Luca: Very interesting...

00:45:01.888 --> 00:45:03.966
it's about the history of philosophy

00:45:03.966 --> 00:45:07.109
and as far as languages are concerned
people usually think

00:45:07.109 --> 00:45:12.868
ok, a polyglot who speaks many languages
has to learn everyday

00:45:12.868 --> 00:45:20.143
the truth is that I don't learn...
I learn one language actively for 30mins

00:45:20.143 --> 00:45:25.947
but then, as you were saying earlier,

00:45:25.947 --> 00:45:33.442
what makes a difference ist that I speak 
lots of languages

00:45:33.442 --> 00:45:38.858
because I use lots of languages
I use them for example during the lessons

00:45:38.858 --> 00:45:47.939
I have to speak English, Spanish... 
Portuguese I use once in 3 months or so

00:45:47.939 --> 00:45:53.398
not very often, but in general I speak many
languages everyday because it's the norm

00:45:53.398 --> 00:45:55.427
integrating your languages in your life

00:45:55.427 --> 00:46:00.700
many people ask me: do you have to study
3 or 4 hours a day?

00:46:00.700 --> 00:46:05.764
No, you have to integrate languages
in your life,

00:46:05.764 --> 00:46:13.339
then it gets much easier, it's normal...
In countries such as Luxemburg

00:46:13.339 --> 00:46:19.320
there are many Portuguese people, 
of Portuguese origin, who speak

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:27.076
Portuguese, Letzeburgisch, French at school,
English, German, that's 5 languages

00:46:27.076 --> 00:46:31.724
and for them that's perfectly normal,
they speak 5 languages and it's normal

00:46:31.724 --> 00:46:35.556
"it's normal in my life" but Italians 
for instance do not speak (as many)

00:46:35.556 --> 00:46:41.527
because in Italy people speak Italian,
in the US they speak American English

00:46:41.527 --> 00:46:47.086
and that's why it's not normal to speak 
2-3 languages,

00:46:47.086 --> 00:46:54.395
but there is a part of humanity
who routinely speak 3, 4, 5 languages

00:46:54.395 --> 00:46:56.584
[in English] Ok, well.
We talked -

00:46:56.584 --> 00:47:00.007
we addressed this in Portuguese.

00:47:00.007 --> 00:47:02.115
Now, let's talk in - 

00:47:02.115 --> 00:47:04.647
The next question, we are going to 
address it in English.

00:47:04.647 --> 00:47:07.540
This is about our job, so
that's interesting!

00:47:07.540 --> 00:47:13.562
"How important is mentoring/coaching
in SLA (Second Language Acquisition)

00:47:13.562 --> 00:47:17.759
and what are the qualities of a good tutor?"

00:47:17.759 --> 00:47:22.035
So, Stefano...
Stefano: Wow! What a question!

00:47:22.035 --> 00:47:24.719
That's a broad, broad subject.

00:47:24.719 --> 00:47:27.770
Luca: it's a broad question, but it's
an interesting one!

00:47:27.770 --> 00:47:34.197
Stefano: First of all I'd like to make clear
what we mean by &lt;i&gt;mentor&lt;/i&gt;

00:47:34.197 --> 00:47:35.930
and what we mean by &lt;i&gt;tutor&lt;/i&gt;

00:47:35.930 --> 00:47:38.014
Because I think they are two 
different thing,

00:47:38.014 --> 00:47:39.535
I think a &lt;i&gt;mentor&lt;/i&gt; is like a coach,

00:47:39.535 --> 00:47:41.668
it's more of what you do.

00:47:41.668 --> 00:47:44.104
Whereas a &lt;i&gt;tutor&lt;/i&gt; is more like
language-specific.

00:47:44.104 --> 00:47:46.120
Luca: Right!
Stefano: are we talking about the same thing?

00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:47.813
Are we on the same page?

00:47:47.813 --> 00:47:53.764
Luca: Yeah! But I would say that,
regardless of that,

00:47:53.764 --> 00:47:56.292
yes, it's true. 
you have to make a distinction

00:47:56.292 --> 00:47:59.333
but I think a good &lt;i&gt;language instructor&lt;/i&gt;

00:47:59.333 --> 00:48:01.926
regardless of mentor, tutor, coach...

00:48:01.926 --> 00:48:03.606
Because these are just labels.

00:48:03.606 --> 00:48:07.605
What do you think a good 
language instructor -

00:48:07.605 --> 00:48:10.682
the main features a language instructor
should have, in general?

00:48:10.682 --> 00:48:12.403
But then you can distinguish 
obviously...

00:48:12.403 --> 00:48:16.439
Stefano: I was gonna talk about 
how important it is

00:48:16.439 --> 00:48:21.361
to have a coach, like a coach
for learning languages in general,

00:48:21.361 --> 00:48:26.342
and/or a tutor specifically
for a language.

00:48:26.342 --> 00:48:29.811
Luca: Or it is better to have both!
Stefano: Exactly!

00:48:29.811 --> 00:48:33.131
Luca: That's what I tell all my students.

00:48:33.131 --> 00:48:35.576
As of me, I can direct you and 
give you some instructions

00:48:35.576 --> 00:48:38.482
then you can actually apply this
with the tutor.

00:48:38.482 --> 00:48:42.253
So, why either/or?
It's just this &lt;i&gt;and&lt;/i&gt; that!

00:48:42.253 --> 00:48:46.686
Stefano: So, I think that if you have 
no idea how to start learning a language

00:48:46.686 --> 00:48:49.922
and how to organize your self-study

00:48:49.922 --> 00:48:55.905
or even if you're under a time pressure 
for any reason.

00:48:55.905 --> 00:49:01.327
To learn a given language in 
a given time you need a coach, I think.

00:49:01.327 --> 00:49:06.688
Because you need to be shown how to 
organize your self-study

00:49:06.688 --> 00:49:09.843
and how to start, basically,
from scratch.

00:49:09.843 --> 00:49:13.462
Whereas if you are already comfortable
with self-study

00:49:13.462 --> 00:49:17.072
maybe you have some experience 
doing that

00:49:17.072 --> 00:49:20.880
and you don't have particular pressure
to learn in a given time,

00:49:20.880 --> 00:49:25.330
then you may want to have a 
language-specific tutor

00:49:25.330 --> 00:49:27.870
or, as we just said, both!

00:49:27.870 --> 00:49:31.111
And I think that -

00:49:31.111 --> 00:49:36.111
But I think that you absolutely need 
is other people.

00:49:36.111 --> 00:49:41.068
Like, 100% you need someone else
to accompany you

00:49:41.068 --> 00:49:45.331
in your language learning process
and journey.

00:49:45.331 --> 00:49:51.251
I mean, the golden rule for me is 
&lt;i&gt;NO ISOLATION&lt;/i&gt;

00:49:51.251 --> 00:49:53.977
or, at least, not for a long time.

00:49:53.977 --> 00:49:58.653
So, you can look for a coach
to start learning a new language,

00:49:58.653 --> 00:50:00.215
if you don't know how to start.

00:50:00.215 --> 00:50:04.012
You can look for a tutor for 
language-specific questions.

00:50:04.012 --> 00:50:08.968
And also you can look for other people 
who wanna learn the same language

00:50:08.968 --> 00:50:12.312
and can accompany you on this journey.

00:50:12.312 --> 00:50:14.356
But talking about what a tutor -

00:50:14.356 --> 00:50:19.774
You will probably talk about what a coach 
or a mentor needs to have as qualities

00:50:19.774 --> 00:50:23.439
But I think I'm gonna talk about 
language-specific tutors

00:50:23.439 --> 00:50:25.346
because that's what I do.

00:50:25.346 --> 00:50:29.457
And I think the number one quality is 
&lt;i&gt;flexibility&lt;/i&gt;

00:50:29.457 --> 00:50:33.183
This is so important!

00:50:33.183 --> 00:50:36.939
A good tutor has to meet students
where they're at.

00:50:36.939 --> 00:50:44.311
And being flexible means being able
to adjust the way they teach

00:50:44.311 --> 00:50:48.618
even though we don't like the word &lt;i&gt;teach&lt;/i&gt;,
I know Luca, but let me use this...

00:50:48.618 --> 00:50:57.858
I mean, the way they teach, or the way
they help the students learn a language

00:50:57.858 --> 00:51:07.017
Depending on the student needs, 
preferences, struggles, goals, all these things

00:51:07.017 --> 00:51:13.537
It's not as easy as it seems but with
some experience you get to do that

00:51:13.537 --> 00:51:15.822
And also, empathy.

00:51:15.822 --> 00:51:19.961
Empathy is extremely important because 
it allows you to -

00:51:19.961 --> 00:51:25.188
to feel in the shoes of your students,

00:51:25.188 --> 00:51:33.603
and really almost feel the same struggle,
the same need that the student is feeling.

00:51:33.603 --> 00:51:38.881
So that you can better address these needs

00:51:38.881 --> 00:51:42.047
Also, I think, experience in self-study.

00:51:42.047 --> 00:51:48.010
When the tutor has himself, or herself, 
learned a foreign language

00:51:48.010 --> 00:51:50.610
or multiple foreign languages, why not.

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:54.668
That's a great thing because you're
pretty sure that they know what it takes

00:51:54.668 --> 00:52:01.288
and why is someone struggling with 
a certain aspect of a language.

00:52:01.288 --> 00:52:06.278
And also, maybe knowing how and when
to correct

00:52:06.278 --> 00:52:13.202
that's more important than it seems, 
because I think many people

00:52:13.202 --> 00:52:18.860
do not understand how important it is 
not to correct too much,

00:52:18.860 --> 00:52:22.371
not to interrupt students 
when they're speaking

00:52:22.371 --> 00:52:29.366
and also, to adjust the level of correction
depending on the level of the student.

00:52:29.366 --> 00:52:34.821
Obviously, if you're a beginner, 
I'm not gonna correct every single thing,

00:52:34.821 --> 00:52:37.571
every single mistake, but only the 
main ones

00:52:37.571 --> 00:52:44.083
because we want to address them and 
correct them to improve fast.

00:52:44.083 --> 00:52:50.321
But if a student is already advanced, 
I'm gonna be much more specific

00:52:50.321 --> 00:52:52.880
and correct much more, 
even the details.

00:52:52.880 --> 00:52:58.462
Especially if I know that the student wants
to speak correctly

00:52:58.462 --> 00:53:01.423
and this is among his/her goals.

00:53:01.423 --> 00:53:06.364
If not, we just chat, and I just give 
an opportunity to express

00:53:06.364 --> 00:53:09.513
and use the language,
and practice it.

00:53:09.513 --> 00:53:11.551
And that's pretty much it!

00:53:11.551 --> 00:53:13.563
Luca: Excellent!
Stefano, you're the man!

00:53:13.563 --> 00:53:17.272
[in Italian] Stefano: Look who's talking!

00:53:17.272 --> 00:53:19.932
Luca: Look who's talking!

00:53:19.932 --> 00:53:22.599
Ok, well... First, as far as I'm concerned

00:53:22.599 --> 00:53:24.478
you almost said it all.

00:53:24.478 --> 00:53:26.710
I think that the features 
that you mentioned

00:53:26.710 --> 00:53:28.853
also apply to a coach.

00:53:28.853 --> 00:53:33.291
Just one thing that you said,
that's really interesting is

00:53:33.291 --> 00:53:36.157
the fact that I always tell my students

00:53:36.157 --> 00:53:40.514
that they have to find a tutor 
when they start practicing a language

00:53:40.514 --> 00:53:43.234
that it would be better if the tutor knows

00:53:43.234 --> 00:53:48.375
what kind of feedback,
how to deliver it, and when

00:53:48.375 --> 00:53:52.416
it's a combination of these three
factors: what, how, and when.

00:53:52.416 --> 00:53:57.247
And this is within the flexibility
feature that you mentioned before

00:53:57.247 --> 00:53:59.088
For me, that's number one.

00:53:59.088 --> 00:54:05.563
Is that a person is flexible,
whether you're a coach or an instructor

00:54:05.563 --> 00:54:08.306
or you're a tutor, or whatever
you want to call it.

00:54:08.306 --> 00:54:10.668
Because being flexible means that

00:54:10.668 --> 00:54:14.915
if you walk into a conversation,
into a program for a student,

00:54:14.915 --> 00:54:19.248
with a set mind that this is gonna apply
to everyone...

00:54:19.248 --> 00:54:23.646
Then you're about to fail.

00:54:23.646 --> 00:54:28.669
This training is destined to fail
because everyone has different taste,

00:54:28.669 --> 00:54:30.084
different background.

00:54:30.084 --> 00:54:33.551
So, flexibility in terms of everything

00:54:33.551 --> 00:54:35.233
in terms of how you deliver 
the feedback,

00:54:35.233 --> 00:54:37.211
in terms of how you adjust to the student,

00:54:37.211 --> 00:54:40.200
in terms of the goals of the students,

00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:44.321
how they speak, how they learn
the language, their taste, you name it...

00:54:44.321 --> 00:54:45.994
That's the very first thing.

00:54:45.994 --> 00:54:47.973
The second thing is &lt;i&gt;humanity&lt;/i&gt;.

00:54:47.973 --> 00:54:52.551
For me competence is important;
you have to be competent.

00:54:52.551 --> 00:54:56.415
But that's a condition you should ???
So to speak...

00:54:56.415 --> 00:55:01.243
But I think that being humane means 
being a nice being.

00:55:01.243 --> 00:55:04.497
You were using the word &lt;i&gt;empathy&lt;/i&gt;
which is a wonderful word.

00:55:04.497 --> 00:55:08.273
You have to feel the other,
feel the struggle.

00:55:08.273 --> 00:55:11.046
You have to be able to connect 
with the student.

00:55:11.046 --> 00:55:13.722
That's really important!

00:55:13.722 --> 00:55:17.661
And that is for me singlehandedly
the most important thing

00:55:17.661 --> 00:55:23.053
And for me, a good coach asks, and
knows how to ask questions.

00:55:23.053 --> 00:55:27.812
I believe more and more that
all the answers are in us

00:55:27.812 --> 00:55:32.278
and you just need to spur the student
in the right way,

00:55:32.278 --> 00:55:35.340
so that they find the answers that 
are already in them.

00:55:35.340 --> 00:55:39.678
So, I think that a good coach
understands the situation

00:55:39.678 --> 00:55:42.604
a good coach has the experience,
understands who they're talking to

00:55:42.604 --> 00:55:47.351
and asks the right questions
so that they cause the students

00:55:47.351 --> 00:55:50.940
to think about solutions that they can
figure out

00:55:50.940 --> 00:55:57.053
and, in this regard, I think that 
a good coach is solution-inclined

00:55:57.053 --> 00:56:00.267
meaning that they never see problems,

00:56:00.267 --> 00:56:04.441
they just see situations that 
need to be resolved.

00:56:04.441 --> 00:56:09.449
So, if someone comes to me and I have 
extensive experience with coaching

00:56:09.449 --> 00:56:11.919
so, people come from all walks of life, 
you know?

00:56:11.919 --> 00:56:14.319
From every country in the world,
I've worked with-

00:56:14.319 --> 00:56:16.683
not everybody, but a lot of people!

00:56:16.683 --> 00:56:20.578
They come with different problems, 
they have different goals and dreams

00:56:20.578 --> 00:56:22.217
and struggles.

00:56:22.217 --> 00:56:26.175
And you just have to identify the problem.

00:56:26.175 --> 00:56:29.447
The more experience you have 
the easier it is to categorize

00:56:29.447 --> 00:56:32.828
certain aspects or certain problems.

00:56:32.828 --> 00:56:35.400
But then what makes the difference
is to ask the right questions.

00:56:35.400 --> 00:56:38.942
Like, instead of telling them:
"This is what you need to do"

00:56:38.942 --> 00:56:42.865
I can suggest a few things, 
but I think it would be best

00:56:42.865 --> 00:56:47.516
to just point to a possible solution 
and ask them -

00:56:47.516 --> 00:56:50.784
direct their brains to figure out
a solution themselves

00:56:50.784 --> 00:56:55.380
because, once again, the best answers
are already in you.

00:56:55.380 --> 00:56:57.069
You just need to find them!

00:56:57.069 --> 00:56:59.021
Stefano: This is fantastic!
This is spectacular!

00:56:59.021 --> 00:57:02.875
I liked your third and fourth points.
Very much, very much!

00:57:02.875 --> 00:57:06.693
Almost Socratic, I would say.
Almost Socratic!

00:57:06.693 --> 00:57:09.496
Luca: I mean, Socrate was a fantastic guy.

00:57:09.496 --> 00:57:14.195
You know, the Socratic method, 
this famous greek philosopher

00:57:14.195 --> 00:57:16.336
who kept asking questions...

00:57:16.336 --> 00:57:18.020
He knew that he didn't know anything

00:57:18.020 --> 00:57:20.211
but his method was great!

00:57:20.211 --> 00:57:22.908
Ask a question, then ask another question
about the question

00:57:22.908 --> 00:57:26.590
and then keep digging, and I feel that
&lt;i&gt;keep digging&lt;/i&gt; is one of the most

00:57:26.590 --> 00:57:28.170
important things we want to do

00:57:28.170 --> 00:57:30.521
as human beings, not only for 
language learning.

00:57:30.521 --> 00:57:34.334
And not only to explore what 
method works best to learn a language

00:57:34.334 --> 00:57:36.933
but to understand the nature of ourselves.

00:57:36.933 --> 00:57:40.906
To understand ourselves, I think it's one 
of the first steps towards a happy life.

00:57:40.906 --> 00:57:43.633
If you want to put it in 
philosophical terms!

00:57:43.633 --> 00:57:46.460
Now the last question

00:57:46.460 --> 00:57:49.788
of the six languages we share together

00:57:49.788 --> 00:57:52.764
and that is the following

00:57:52.764 --> 00:57:55.784
and we're gonna reply in beautiful Spanish

00:57:55.784 --> 00:57:58.898
What brings the most joy in language learning?

00:57:58.898 --> 00:58:00.581
It is a very simple question, beautiful.

00:58:00.581 --> 00:58:02.341
[in Spanish] Stefano: very well

00:58:02.341 --> 00:58:03.773
Luca: Ok, very well, let's get started.

00:58:03.773 --> 00:58:04.820
Stefano: Let's get started,

00:58:04.820 --> 00:58:07.587
but you need to help me with Spanish Luca,

00:58:07.587 --> 00:58:09.451
Luca: I'll help you, I'll help you

00:58:09.451 --> 00:58:13.323
because my Spanish is very very rusty

00:58:13.323 --> 00:58:16.860
It has always been this way and forever it will be!

00:58:16.860 --> 00:58:23.260
For me, well it has a little bit to do

00:58:23.260 --> 00:58:26.847
with what we already said about motivation.

00:58:26.847 --> 00:58:33.009
But, one thing that gives me "jewels"

00:58:33.009 --> 00:58:35.777
Do you say it like this, "jewels"?

00:58:35.777 --> 00:58:38.474
Luca: I would say "that gives me pleasure"

00:58:38.474 --> 00:58:42.207
It gives me great pleasure, so that it makes me happy

00:58:42.207 --> 00:58:47.020
Stefano: Did you say something?
Luca: No no nothing, continue

00:58:47.020 --> 00:58:49.001
Stefano: what gives me pleasure

00:58:49.001 --> 00:58:51.735
is the epiphany.

00:58:51.735 --> 00:58:54.471
This kind of enlightenment, you know

00:58:54.471 --> 00:58:59.516
The fact that something with no meaning

00:58:59.516 --> 00:59:02.764
until recently,

00:59:02.764 --> 00:59:04.997
all of a sudden changes into

00:59:04.997 --> 00:59:07.711
a message you understand

00:59:07.711 --> 00:59:10.657
into something with what you can inter..

00:59:10.657 --> 00:59:12.387
I don't know how to say it

00:59:12.387 --> 00:59:13.875
Luca: "interact"

00:59:13.875 --> 00:59:15.209
Stefano: interact, perfect.

00:59:15.209 --> 00:59:20.436
This a very special thing, a very special moment

00:59:20.436 --> 00:59:22.063
in (language) learning, I think.

00:59:22.063 --> 00:59:25.135
And another thing is, well,

00:59:25.135 --> 00:59:28.282
the simple fact to express yourself

00:59:28.282 --> 00:59:34.703
to have an exchange of ideas, of opinions

00:59:34.703 --> 00:59:37.380
with other persons

00:59:37.380 --> 00:59:42.586
with whom you probably wouldn't talk

00:59:42.586 --> 00:59:45.910
with whom you probably wouldn't have an exchange

00:59:45.910 --> 00:59:48.867
if you didn't speak that language.

00:59:48.867 --> 00:59:50.591
Luca: Of course.

00:59:50.591 --> 00:59:52.968
Stefano: And if not, also the simple fact

00:59:52.968 --> 00:59:54.712
of helping others

00:59:54.712 --> 00:59:57.133
It gives me great pleasure to help others

00:59:57.133 --> 01:00:00.760
Not only as a tutor

01:00:00.760 --> 01:00:04.258
not only as someone who helps other people

01:00:04.258 --> 01:00:09.150
learning a language, but also when...

01:00:09.150 --> 01:00:13.610
I don't know, on the street, in similar situations,

01:00:13.610 --> 01:00:16.520
there are people who have a problem

01:00:16.520 --> 01:00:20.653
and cannot solve it

01:00:20.653 --> 01:00:23.381
because of a lack of a common language

01:00:23.381 --> 01:00:26.638
I did it a lot of times here in Brussels

01:00:26.638 --> 01:00:28.174
Luca: Me too.

01:00:28.174 --> 01:00:31.909
Stefano: at the market and thus

01:00:31.909 --> 01:00:36.492
It's also a very special feeling.

01:00:36.492 --> 01:00:39.093
And if not, there's another thing,

01:00:39.093 --> 01:00:43.386
which is a little bit weird,

01:00:43.386 --> 01:00:45.786
and that's when I have a level

01:00:45.786 --> 01:00:48.113
which is high or good enough

01:00:48.113 --> 01:00:52.801
and I can listen to stand-up comedy

01:00:52.801 --> 01:00:55.673
I don't know how to say it in Spanish

01:00:55.673 --> 01:01:05.407
Luca: just stand-up comedian, comic comicalness

01:01:05.407 --> 01:01:09.159
Stefano: also a special thing is

01:01:09.159 --> 01:01:11.995
understand and laugh with a guffaw

01:01:11.995 --> 01:01:13.728
and it's a double pleasure,

01:01:13.728 --> 01:01:19.957
because there's the pleasure of... the joke?

01:01:19.957 --> 01:01:22.536
Do you say it like this? Yes? The joke

01:01:22.536 --> 01:01:25.767
Of course, but also to know that

01:01:25.767 --> 01:01:29.823
you understood something in another language

01:01:29.823 --> 01:01:31.956
because it's a joke in another language

01:01:31.956 --> 01:01:35.706
so you have the double pleasure of...

01:01:35.706 --> 01:01:38.878
Stefano: The pleasure of the joke
and the linguistic pleasure.

01:01:38.878 --> 01:01:41.634
Let's say it like this. And that's it.

01:01:41.634 --> 01:01:44.853
Luca: Fantastic, fantastic.

01:01:44.853 --> 01:01:47.589
As for me, well, along with all the things

01:01:47.589 --> 01:01:50.424
you just said, there are 3 main things

01:01:50.424 --> 01:01:54.576
that represent for me an absolute pleasure

01:01:54.576 --> 01:01:56.875
when I'm talking a foreign language

01:01:56.875 --> 01:01:59.631
The first thing is, just like you,

01:01:59.631 --> 01:02:01.656
to communicate with the others.

01:02:01.656 --> 01:02:03.896
To talk with other persons, and not only

01:02:03.896 --> 01:02:06.242
to talk with the intention to communicate,

01:02:06.242 --> 01:02:09.264
but also to talk with natives about traditions,

01:02:09.264 --> 01:02:11.945
history, movies, literature

01:02:11.945 --> 01:02:14.687
of their culture, of their country.

01:02:14.687 --> 01:02:18.581
It is a great intellectual accomplishment

01:02:18.581 --> 01:02:20.853
you know, to simply communicate with other people

01:02:20.853 --> 01:02:24.110
without resorting to English.

01:02:24.110 --> 01:02:26.228
That's already amazing.

01:02:26.228 --> 01:02:30.184
Secondly, it was an absolute pleasure for me,

01:02:30.184 --> 01:02:31.649
as I already told you before,

01:02:31.649 --> 01:02:36.583
that when I went to Spain, Barcelona and France,

01:02:36.583 --> 01:02:38.907
knowing how to speak those languages beforehand

01:02:38.907 --> 01:02:41.056
knowing those languages beforehand

01:02:41.056 --> 01:02:42.587
was an incredible advantage

01:02:42.587 --> 01:02:45.133
because moving to another country

01:02:45.133 --> 01:02:47.256
is sometimes a little bit stressful

01:02:47.256 --> 01:02:49.675
It is stressful because sometimes you need to call

01:02:49.675 --> 01:02:51.143
and there's a different system

01:02:51.143 --> 01:02:53.818
and you need to make calculations, understand numbers and so on and so forth

01:02:53.818 --> 01:02:56.570
and being able to speak the local language makes one's life easier

01:02:56.570 --> 01:02:58.352
in an incredible way.

01:02:58.352 --> 01:03:02.912
It's a stressful situation and it's a pleasure
being able to do it

01:03:02.912 --> 01:03:04.727
and afterwards you're saying "Jesus Christ",

01:03:04.727 --> 01:03:07.565
Thank God I speak this language - everything is easier this way!

01:03:07.565 --> 01:03:10.015
You can interact with the rest, 
with the people,

01:03:10.015 --> 01:03:14.031
in stores, you can get your 
phone and rent something...

01:03:14.031 --> 01:03:18.472
For me, it's a pleasure because it makes

01:03:18.472 --> 01:03:21.277
the overall experience becomes much richer

01:03:21.277 --> 01:03:24.045
It's incredible.

01:03:24.045 --> 01:03:30.327
Third, as you said, talking with natives

01:03:30.327 --> 01:03:34.076
brings surprise, a reaction from their side

01:03:34.076 --> 01:03:36.093
Sometimes... For example, I'll give you 2 things:

01:03:36.093 --> 01:03:38.823
One thing is often on the street

01:03:38.823 --> 01:03:40.438
You meet a person who is lost

01:03:40.438 --> 01:03:42.882
or a group of people

01:03:42.882 --> 01:03:45.833
I remember one, one and a half year ago

01:03:45.833 --> 01:03:48.286
when I met a group of Polish people

01:03:48.286 --> 01:03:50.918
who didn't know how to get to the Vatican,

01:03:50.918 --> 01:03:52.657
who didn't know where to go

01:03:52.657 --> 01:03:55.245
and suddenly - because when you're in a group,

01:03:55.245 --> 01:03:57.823
you don't have to ask where they come from

01:03:57.823 --> 01:03:59.868
because they reveal their language by talking to each other,

01:03:59.868 --> 01:04:03.074
you already know it, the guy says something
and you know "ah, it's a Polish man"

01:04:03.074 --> 01:04:05.493
and suddenly you start talking in Polish

01:04:05.493 --> 01:04:06.870
and they are surprised and go like

01:04:06.870 --> 01:04:08.647
"Wow, do you speak Polish? How so?"

01:04:08.647 --> 01:04:13.576
Their reaction is an incredible reward in and of itself

01:04:13.576 --> 01:04:17.260
I'll tell you a story from two months ago

01:04:17.260 --> 01:04:19.327
regarding the pandemic

01:04:19.327 --> 01:04:23.376
What happened is that on the 6th or 7th of March,

01:04:23.376 --> 01:04:29.234
some days before the (Italian) government decided

01:04:29.234 --> 01:04:32.390
to close the borders, there were 2 people

01:04:32.390 --> 01:04:35.529
in the grocery store who were asking something

01:04:35.529 --> 01:04:40.420
they were trying to... there was a girl and a boy

01:04:40.420 --> 01:04:42.899
and they were asking something

01:04:42.899 --> 01:04:47.579
I saw it, I saw that they were trying to communicate

01:04:47.579 --> 01:04:51.025
in English, but not in good English,

01:04:51.025 --> 01:04:55.159
and besides, the people themselves from the grocery store

01:04:55.159 --> 01:04:56.662
didn't understand anything,

01:04:56.662 --> 01:04:57.984
because they didn't speak English,

01:04:57.984 --> 01:05:01.961
and that's why I approached them and I asked

01:05:01.961 --> 01:05:05.364
"May I lend a hand?"

01:05:05.364 --> 01:05:08.666
and afterwards I asked "What language do you speak

01:05:08.666 --> 01:05:10.557
what is your mother tongue?"

01:05:10.557 --> 01:05:13.404
and when the girl answered "Hungarian"

01:05:13.404 --> 01:05:15.439
and I started to talk in Hungarian,

01:05:15.439 --> 01:05:17.188
she was flabbergasted

01:05:17.188 --> 01:05:19.391
"Wow, you speak Hungarian!?!"

01:05:19.391 --> 01:05:22.053
because you see, one thing is speaking Hungarian in Budapest,

01:05:22.053 --> 01:05:24.937
when you're in Hungary, natives will go like

01:05:24.937 --> 01:05:26.717
"Ok, you're speak Hungarian, it's something, but no big deal

01:05:26.717 --> 01:05:28.557
if you're in Hungary.

01:05:28.557 --> 01:05:32.604
But to bump into a guy who speaks Hungarian abroad

01:05:32.604 --> 01:05:35.944
all of a sudden... It's their reaction

01:05:35.944 --> 01:05:41.187
and besides, they were asking where to buy bus tickets

01:05:41.187 --> 01:05:45.236
because everything's closed Sunday night in Rome,

01:05:45.236 --> 01:05:48.952
and you can't find bus tickets - like in other countries.

01:05:48.952 --> 01:05:52.107
You know, let's put it this way: the overall ticket sale system is way better

01:05:52.107 --> 01:05:54.392
in other countries, because (differently from Italy) you can

01:05:54.392 --> 01:05:57.819
buy a ticket wherever (and whenever) you want.

01:05:57.819 --> 01:06:02.194
That's why I took some tickets in my wallet,

01:06:02.194 --> 01:06:04.824
and I gave it to them and therefore

01:06:04.824 --> 01:06:06.492
I was a guy who spoke Hungarian,

01:06:06.492 --> 01:06:08.395
who gave those two tickets for free,

01:06:08.395 --> 01:06:10.301
- because I didn't want any money from them -

01:06:10.301 --> 01:06:12.580
and I returned home with a lot more motivation

01:06:12.580 --> 01:06:16.606
to learn Hungarian, because the fact that

01:06:16.606 --> 01:06:19.389
you could communicate, that you could help somebody

01:06:19.389 --> 01:06:21.976
gives you a huge reward, it gives you happiness.

01:06:21.976 --> 01:06:23.402
Especially if it's a language like Hungarian,

01:06:23.402 --> 01:06:25.896
which is not a common language, you know.

01:06:25.896 --> 01:06:33.506
So I returned home with a lot of motivation

01:06:33.506 --> 01:06:36.460
and now it makes even more sense to learn Hungarian!

01:06:36.460 --> 01:06:37.900
Sometimes you're asking yourself

01:06:37.900 --> 01:06:40.238
why am I learning Hungarian?!

01:06:40.238 --> 01:06:42.980
and other more exotic languages

01:06:42.980 --> 01:06:46.393
and the answer to this question 
is right there

01:06:46.393 --> 01:06:48.006
Stefano: it's there!

01:06:48.006 --> 01:06:50.497
Luca: yes, it's there. And there are always

01:06:50.497 --> 01:06:52.042
plenty of possibilities to use the language

01:06:52.042 --> 01:06:54.337
that we don't see and suddenly life gives them to us

01:06:54.337 --> 01:06:57.909
the possibility to see the meaning of

01:06:57.909 --> 01:07:00.898
communication in other languages.

01:07:00.898 --> 01:07:03.315
It's incredible, it's a reward in itself.

01:07:03.315 --> 01:07:06.427
I wanted to tell this story, because, well, that was a cool story to tell!

01:07:06.427 --> 01:07:10.513
[in English] Ok, now.
We share six languages.

01:07:10.513 --> 01:07:13.880
But you speak at least a dozen,
and I speak a dozen.

01:07:13.880 --> 01:07:18.864
So, we thought about creating 
some bonus material

01:07:18.864 --> 01:07:21.065
and that's a seventh question.

01:07:21.065 --> 01:07:24.096
Now, we're going to address-

01:07:24.096 --> 01:07:26.586
I'm gonna address it in a language
that you don't know

01:07:26.586 --> 01:07:29.468
and you're going to address it
in a language that I don't know.

01:07:29.468 --> 01:07:34.713
And so, if you see me looking
at you but not understanding

01:07:34.713 --> 01:07:38.563
it's because I don't understand Finnish.

01:07:38.563 --> 01:07:40.360
That's the language you're 
gonna speak, right?

01:07:40.360 --> 01:07:42.261
Stefano: Right!

01:07:42.261 --> 01:07:45.735
The same goes for your language.

01:07:45.735 --> 01:07:50.859
Luca: Yes. My language is going
to be Polish, so...

01:07:50.859 --> 01:07:54.141
??? of me, I'm gonna listen to 
your beautiful Finnish

01:07:54.141 --> 01:07:57.614
but I will not understand what you say

01:07:57.614 --> 01:08:00.794
Stefano: But please ask the question
in English!

01:08:00.794 --> 01:08:03.701
Luca: Right!
That's what I'm gonna do in a second!

01:08:03.701 --> 01:08:08.570
So, for those who understand Finnish
that's great!

01:08:08.570 --> 01:08:11.570
But, anyway, we'll have the subtitles
in English...

01:08:11.570 --> 01:08:16.444
And finally, I will understand 
in a little bit what he said.

01:08:16.444 --> 01:08:18.662
When the video gets published!

01:08:18.662 --> 01:08:20.722
Ok, so, without further ado...

01:08:20.722 --> 01:08:24.102
Here is the question. 
I'm gonna ask it in English:

01:08:24.102 --> 01:08:28.232
"How to squeeze the best out
of your learning session?"

01:08:28.232 --> 01:08:30.549
And I think that the person asking 
this question-

01:08:30.549 --> 01:08:32.350
I don't know whether 
they had in mind

01:08:32.350 --> 01:08:34.160
your learning session with a tutor

01:08:34.160 --> 01:08:36.410
or your learning session in general...

01:08:36.410 --> 01:08:39.434
I understood like, 
your learning session-

01:08:39.434 --> 01:08:41.400
you're sitting down by yourself
and learn, so...

01:08:41.400 --> 01:08:43.398
That was my understanding

01:08:43.398 --> 01:08:45.333
and this is how we are going 
to reply.

01:08:45.333 --> 01:08:48.302
please, go ahead with your
beautiful Finnish!

01:08:48.767 --> 01:08:51.092
Thank you! 
Thank you, Luca

01:08:51.092 --> 01:08:54.067
what a joy to speak Finnish
even though you can't understand

01:08:54.067 --> 01:08:57.477
well, this is a bit weird [laughter]

01:08:57.477 --> 01:09:00.841
So, first of all, it seems to me that

01:09:00.841 --> 01:09:04.110
we need consistency and regularity

01:09:04.110 --> 01:09:06.695
because you get a good rhythm out of them

01:09:06.695 --> 01:09:08.846
and when you have a good rhythm

01:09:08.846 --> 01:09:13.796
our brain kinda absorbs 
information much better

01:09:14.692 --> 01:09:19.403
because it knows: now comes the time

01:09:19.405 --> 01:09:24.945
where I'm gonna study my (target) language

01:09:24.976 --> 01:09:29.499
and therefore it's ready to absorb more
of what we study

01:09:29.600 --> 01:09:32.375
Then, concentration as well...

01:09:32.375 --> 01:09:36.268
I'm pretty sure Luca is going to say
the same thing

01:09:36.344 --> 01:09:38.620
because I know him

01:09:38.638 --> 01:09:42.604
even though he has no clue about what 
I'm saying right now

01:09:42.655 --> 01:09:47.069
But concentration is very, very important

01:09:47.092 --> 01:09:50.522
So, for example, switch off your mobile

01:09:50.551 --> 01:09:56.516
or as an alternative,
if your study session is on your phone

01:09:56.516 --> 01:10:00.499
you can also use one of those apps

01:10:00.501 --> 01:10:05.403
what was its name again? 
Was it Peace of Mind? [sorry wrong name!]

01:10:05.433 --> 01:10:11.303
Such apps stop all incoming messages

01:10:11.329 --> 01:10:18.044
but you can still keep using your phone

01:10:18.092 --> 01:10:20.942
so that can be very useful, too.

01:10:21.008 --> 01:10:30.407
What else? Yep, write down words, 
ideally on paper

01:10:30.602 --> 01:10:36.364
but in any case not just words alone, 
but also sentences

01:10:36.414 --> 01:10:39.753
that is, context is so key

01:10:39.786 --> 01:10:43.849
and if you use (apps like) ANKI

01:10:43.953 --> 01:10:50.763
there as well you can... 
well, it'd be best to write full sentences

01:10:50.966 --> 01:11:00.778
because they will give you the context 
that you need in order to learn better

01:11:02.266 --> 01:11:06.727
and obviously if you are speaking 
in your study session

01:11:06.839 --> 01:11:12.478
try and use in the new conversation

01:11:12.666 --> 01:11:19.570
the expressions you've learnt recently

01:11:19.570 --> 01:11:24.920
because that is how they will stick 
in your head

01:11:25.114 --> 01:11:31.251
by actually using them multiple times
each and every time

01:11:31.349 --> 01:11:34.451
Erm, what else...

01:11:34.511 --> 01:11:39.771
Well, maybe the most important thing is:
just have fun!

01:11:39.784 --> 01:11:42.045
This is so important!

01:11:42.095 --> 01:11:44.668
You know, your study session...

01:11:44.668 --> 01:11:48.887
... those 30 minutes or 60 minutes

01:11:48.901 --> 01:11:53.721
that's your own personal time you get
to spend with the language you like

01:11:53.721 --> 01:11:55.927
with the language you love

01:11:55.927 --> 01:11:58.938
so be happy, be grateful

01:11:59.135 --> 01:12:03.047
for having the opportunity
to sit there and learn it

01:12:03.148 --> 01:12:08.307
and just have fun! 
That's all :)

01:12:08.479 --> 01:12:10.224
Thank you!

01:12:10.465 --> 01:12:13.298
Luca: Fantastic! How do you say 
"fantastic" in Finnish?

01:12:13.298 --> 01:12:16.533
Stefano: "Mahtavaa!"
Luca: "Mahtavaa!"

01:12:16.603 --> 01:12:19.282
Ok, now it's my turn
and I am going to speak in Polish

01:12:19.380 --> 01:12:22.490
I'm gonna speak Polish now, obviously.

01:12:22.490 --> 01:12:27.756
First of all, I think it's very important, if you want to become an efficient learner

01:12:27.756 --> 01:12:29.391
first and foremost

01:12:29.391 --> 01:12:32.783
you have to plan out everything in detail.

01:12:32.783 --> 01:12:35.410
It means that when you plan it out

01:12:35.410 --> 01:12:37.663
you will know what things you need to do,

01:12:37.663 --> 01:12:42.293
how and when to do them, and that's just great

01:12:42.293 --> 01:12:45.054
For instance, I know exactly

01:12:45.054 --> 01:12:49.908
which language I'm going to study daily,

01:12:49.908 --> 01:12:51.497
how long it will take me,

01:12:51.497 --> 01:12:53.102
which method I will apply, etc.

01:12:53.127 --> 01:12:55.116
In the case of Polish for instance

01:12:55.116 --> 01:12:57.229
I listen to podcasts on a daily basis -

01:12:57.229 --> 01:13:00.737
when I take a shower or a bath.

01:13:00.737 --> 01:13:03.592
And when it comes to Hungarian

01:13:03.592 --> 01:13:09.136
I read interesting articles every day, etc.

01:13:09.136 --> 01:13:11.194
And in the case of Greek

01:13:11.194 --> 01:13:14.887
I take a conversation class once a week,

01:13:14.887 --> 01:13:16.084
for example.

01:13:16.084 --> 01:13:17.952
And I study each day,

01:13:17.952 --> 01:13:21.344
watch videos on YouTube, etc.

01:13:21.344 --> 01:13:22.191
Secondly,

01:13:22.191 --> 01:13:23.993
your learning environment

01:13:23.993 --> 01:13:26.074
is very important.

01:13:26.074 --> 01:13:29.224
It's really, really, really important.

01:13:29.224 --> 01:13:32.350
In my opinion, it's crucial, for instance,

01:13:32.350 --> 01:13:36.325
that you remove all the elements

01:13:36.325 --> 01:13:39.315
that may distract you.

01:13:39.315 --> 01:13:40.994
You may find it too obvious

01:13:40.994 --> 01:13:42.631
what I'm gonna say,

01:13:42.631 --> 01:13:45.465
but please make sure that

01:13:45.465 --> 01:13:47.296
your mobile phone is switched off

01:13:47.296 --> 01:13:49.345
and your desk is clean and tidy.

01:13:49.345 --> 01:13:53.902
Before I introduced those rules

01:13:53.902 --> 01:13:55.619
it was a mess.

01:13:55.619 --> 01:14:00.246
The same goes for the desktop of your PC.

01:14:00.246 --> 01:14:01.631
It's the same thing.

01:14:01.631 --> 01:14:06.235
And make sure you leave a maximum of one or two tabs open

01:14:06.235 --> 01:14:08.848
which is very important for your concentration

01:14:08.848 --> 01:14:11.043
for your attention etc etc

01:14:11.043 --> 01:14:13.596
You can even say it's a key factor.

01:14:13.596 --> 01:14:17.670
Yes, I can see you understand everything
what I'm saying.

01:14:17.670 --> 01:14:21.275
And to my mind the Internet

01:14:21.275 --> 01:14:24.900
is a perfect source of information.

01:14:24.900 --> 01:14:30.500
But in my humble opinion

01:14:30.500 --> 01:14:33.108
also paper plays a significant role here.

01:14:33.108 --> 01:14:38.286
That's why I always try and work with paper.

01:14:38.286 --> 01:14:42.692
And if you read a printed book or an article

01:14:42.692 --> 01:14:45.935
you can take notes on the pages

01:14:45.935 --> 01:14:47.970
or underline words and so forth.

01:14:47.970 --> 01:14:51.995
And there are other things that you can do.

01:14:51.995 --> 01:14:55.604
But in general, I think staying focused

01:14:55.604 --> 01:14:58.405
plays an enormous role

01:14:58.405 --> 01:15:02.692
We are used to using our mobile phones

01:15:02.692 --> 01:15:06.051
but I think you can also get used to

01:15:06.051 --> 01:15:08.074
working with paper,

01:15:08.074 --> 01:15:11.297
which will boost your learning process.

01:15:11.297 --> 01:15:14.616
And remember to study regularly,

01:15:14.616 --> 01:15:18.203
it can be as little as 5 or 10 minutes a day

01:15:18.203 --> 01:15:20.791
and you will see that you'll know exactly

01:15:20.791 --> 01:15:23.779
when, how, and what to do every day.

01:15:23.779 --> 01:15:25.132
That's it, that was all.

01:15:25.132 --> 01:15:26.738
[in English] That was all!

01:15:26.738 --> 01:15:28.852
Stefano (in Polish): Great, great

01:15:28.852 --> 01:15:34.253
Luca [in Polish]: very well

01:15:34.253 --> 01:15:41.990
Luca: you can say "zajebiscie", "spoko", "mega" (slang words for "cool")

01:15:41.990 --> 01:15:43.123
Ok guys, that was it!

01:15:43.123 --> 01:15:44.825
I hope you had fun, I hope you
liked the interview!

01:15:44.825 --> 01:15:47.426
I found it very nice and always very
informative to talk

01:15:47.426 --> 01:15:49.158
with the legendary Stefano.

01:15:49.158 --> 01:15:52.216
Stefano: It was a pleasure!

